sections.append(('readings','The two readings', '''<div class="st-reading"><p class="kicker">Reading A · Thomas’s hypothesis · Unproven identification</p><h3>A separate travel identity</h3><p>Thomas’s hypothesis is that Barak traveled the commercial leg under a separate identity — “Ronen Cohen” — so no airline manifest would carry his name into Epstein’s island gateway. It explains the anomaly register economically: the exact flight, zero footprint, “TEST” subject, class of travel and absence of a Barak-named ticket for a trip he provably took. This is an inference, not a passenger identification established by a manifest, passport, boarding record or agency account.</p></div>
<div class="st-reading"><p class="kicker">Reading B · Alternative explanation · Unproven identification</p><h3>A real associate on a parallel itinerary</h3><p>Ronen Cohen could be a real associate — security, advance or staff — whose trip paralleled the Baraks’ on the same flight, while Barak’s own-name ticket was booked through a channel whose records never entered this mailbox. This explains the return-leg mismatch: Cohen’s booking leaves December 14; the Baraks stayed to December 15. Reading A must treat Cohen’s earlier return as unused or changed, without a record establishing that change.</p><p>Reading B draws real support from the December 9 exchange: a security detail demonstrably accompanied the Baraks’ arrangements and self-booked nearby, so Cohen could be that detail’s paper. Against it, the February 2015 email names the St. Thomas bodyguard as Jarrod Nadel, not Cohen; the research regards security traveling First class home as atypical; and a working protection officer leaving no other trace in 31,718 indexed emails — no located roster, expense thread or cc — remains an anomaly. None of those points excludes a second officer, another staff role, an unrelated same-flight passenger or incomplete mailbox coverage.</p></div>
<div class="fact-box"><p><strong>What the archive can publish today</strong> is the sequence and the anomaly, in full, with both readings labeled. The identification itself is an inference — a strong one, argued from seven documented facts — and GAH’s standard governs its framing: “I would rather downgrade a promising story after checking the evidence than publish an exciting interpretation that the documents cannot sustain.” The documents are the story; the conclusion is the reader’s to draw alongside ours, labeled as ours.</p><p>“Strong” describes Thomas’s assessment, not an independently verified identification. The register above lists eight observations, including the additional hotel-name exchange. Neither reading is established as fact.</p></div>'''))
sections.append(('photos','Photo hunt — negative finding', '''<p>The supplied research reports that two crews searched for photographs of Barak in St. Thomas or on Epstein’s island and found none in the reviewed DOJ corpus or GAH photo collection. It also reports that no reviewed email mentioning St. Thomas, St. John, Caribbean or Virgin Islands carried a photographic image. Within that sweep, the trip window is photographically empty: the trip record presented here is text-only. An unsuccessful search is not proof that no photograph exists elsewhere.</p><p><strong>Excluded image:</strong> the November 1, 2014 Bahamas sailing-catamaran photograph, with EXIF GPS east of Nassau, is not St. Thomas, is not Epstein’s island and has no established Epstein tie. It is not evidence for this trip and is not used on this page or its social card.</p>'''))
sections.append(('questions','What would settle it', '''<ul><li>Any Groff/office logistics naming the December 12 arrival party. EFTA00356456 partially covers this by naming the Baraks at the hotel, but does not identify the airline passenger.</li><li>Hadassim’s account records for PNR 7HLSOQ: who paid, and for whom the “TEST” booking was opened.</li><li>Any second Cohen itinerary or Barak alias candidate in the raw leak attachments, with provenance and identity corroboration.</li><li>Independent records of Barak’s whereabouts December 12–14, 2014: press, Knesset or lecture records.</li></ul>'''))

